From fc34ad4f4aa6e825c9502621ecea900f2c706970 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bakhodirovxz <bohodirovasadbek1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:50:27 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] model: keep a band's cells in one vector

A row held its cells in a vector of its own: a heap allocation per row,
an entry in the band's row map, and (column, cell) pairs padded to 48
bytes. Keep a band's cells in one vector instead, row-major, their
columns in another and an index of where each row starts: 44 bytes per
cell and 8 per row, nothing allocated per row. A band of 64 filled-down
rows of 9 cells costs 404 bytes a row instead of 485.

Moving cells (the shifts, Cut, Insert/Delete) takes the block out of each
band it touches and merges the moved cells into each destination band
once, storing each as `set` would. A band started right after another (a
reader filling a sheet) is sized like it, so loading allocates each band
once: grown cell by cell, its vectors would be reallocated at every
doubling, large blocks that fragment the Windows heap and made reading an
80 MB workbook take 84 s instead of 46. The differential test against the
B-tree store (random sets, removals, shifts, range takes, snapshots,
diffs, JSON) passes unchanged; the JSON form is the same.
